The concrete issues are contract-level, not implementation code: the frozen job handle stores mutable state inside a frozen identity object; a `list` field defaults to a tuple; cancellation callbacks are assigned to a thread that can never invoke them with the current stop mechanism; the partial-result example and derivation conflict; provider-specific settings are simultaneously frozen as request fields; and full exception tracebacks can become execution-record logs despite the egress prohibition. I’m correcting those semantics in the contract, plus tightening retry idempotency so a retried POST cannot silently create duplicate remote jobs.
